WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.740
Film Courage: How do you direct a great scene?

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.479
What are you looking for?

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:04.490
What has to happen?

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.170
Frank Coraci, Writer/Director: How do I direct
a great scene?

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.330
That’s not a fair question [laughs].

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:16.770
You’ve got to direct a scene as best you
can and then sometimes there are moments where

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:21.340
you’re like wow this could be the best scene
in the movie.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:26.670
You just try to service the story the best
you can.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:32.570
So some movies I feel like you want the hand
of the director to push it around and push

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:38.420
the camera and move the camera and other movies
like a romantic comedy like THE WEDDING SINGER

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:44.260
I wanted to just step back and not feel the
director’s hand so on that movie I used

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.370
colors to tell the story.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.420
Adam goes from when he’s happy to blue.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.860
When he gets his heart broken he camera dollies
and you see red brick behind him from that

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.110
point on he is in all maroons and reds and
his heart is broken.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:07.279
At one point he goes to a red wedding this
Italian/silver/black garish and he has the

00:01:07.279 --> 00:01:13.549
Love Stinks scene and he freaks out and he
still has the broken heart of this ex and

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:16.709
starting to see Julia’s happiness again.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:24.039
At the Bar mitzvah he still has the red jacket,
they bond over the little girl and he takes

00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:30.250
off the red jacket because he finally looses
the other girl (the broken heart).

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:34.280
So that was done in a way where you didn’t
feel the director doing it but subconsciously

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.959
it’s there.

00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:43.679
Other movies I like flying the camera around,
different angles and you just try to service

00:01:43.679 --> 00:01:46.520
every scene the best you can.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:54.890
In HOT AIR the indie I just did I had broken
the fourth wall and part of the goal of the

00:01:54.890 --> 00:02:03.729
movie was to not pick sides and then at some
point, we’re not picking right being wrong

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:10.000
or left being wrong but I wanted everyone
in the audience to feel guilty for where we

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.209
are at and there’s a speech toward the end
of the movie where Steve has almost that NETWORK

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:22.159
moment where he breaks down and snaps on everyone
and the writer I said make sure we hit everyone

00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:29.120
and Facebook and Instagram and phones and
he writes this brilliant speech and when I

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.000
shot it it was just sort of happening and
this is going to be great and jump cuts and

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.520
I’m like we’ve got to break to the fourth
wall and the very end of the movie Steve [Coogan]

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.340
looks down the lens and says “The American
Dream is dead and buried and you’re dancing

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.340
on its grave.”

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.079
And this was one of my…I’ve never broke
the fourth wall and I waited for the right

00:02:47.079 --> 00:02:54.540
moment and it’s like you just try to direct
every scene the best you can and then it just

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.500
sort of happens sometimes where you feel like
that was pretty darn good!

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.180
Film Courage: Do you storyboard?

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:06.090
Frank: I shot listed and then if there are
visual effects or it gets complicated I storyboard.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:12.350
It’s time constraints but I literally shotlist
and my goal is literally you shotlist the

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:16.989
movie in order because you read it and you
shotlist it because you read it and shotlist

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:20.170
it so you can evolve lens and do stuff.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:25.269
Whenever I hear the director shows up that
day and decides what lenses unless you’re

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:30.299
shooting in order you’re not telling a real
story.

00:03:30.299 --> 00:03:34.420
To me a movie is a vision from beginning to
end so I will in the beginning let’s start

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.950
on wide lenses so it’s close and intimate
but in the end…things like that you need

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:44.230
to progressively do so you need to plan it
ahead of time so you know you shoot day 31

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.790
- we are on the tight lenses, we’re not
on the wide or the color or the shirt should

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:52.519
be red not…so you have to plan all of that
stuff out in order.

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:58.099
That’s just discipline and sometimes I think
only 10 percent of the directors really do

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:01.790
the job like that and I think it shows.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.819
People that are great are great for a reason.

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:06.819
It takes work.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:13.659
The color thing came into play…my first
movie MURDERED INNOCENCE I literally had like

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:17.560
this guy had to be this guy and that guy,
the guy was just like a construction guy so

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.150
I didn’t have like a like a designer.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:25.970
We were just using locations and I had seen
BILLY MADISON with Sandler and was like “Man,

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.150
the production design is amazing!”

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:32.210
And I’m like “I want that guy.”

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:36.419
And Perry Blake who has done almost all of
my movies because I was like that guy is great

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:39.419
we hit it off because the first movie we did
was WEDDING CRASHERS and I’m like “I want

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:42.370
to tell a story with colors not camera movements.”

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:48.850
So on every movie it’s all about like in
CLICK - he talked me into doing the movie

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.370
- he’s like “You get to create the future.”

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.840
And I’m like “Oh, yeah…wait the story
is great.”

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.710
One part of the future everything is angular
and next because we have a bunch of time jumps

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.150
the later part everything is rounding.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:07.250
Even in just like storytelling color is in
HERE COMES THE BOOM with Kevin James he starts

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:12.710
as a horrible person who is just selfish,
it’s the winter and we made the color - we

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:14.760
pulled all the saturation and made it look
really cool.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:20.479
It felt like THE FIGHTER and then as he blossoms
as a human being and then as a teacher and

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:24.282
starts teaching his kids and we get more and
more colorful and I start adding color, adding

00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:30.150
color to the set and then we actually did
it so the seasons change too.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:34.820
We timed the movie out so we’re shooting
an outdoor scene when the trees were blossoming

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.370
and then it ends in Vegas which was like full
spectacle.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:42.539
So if you see reel one, it looks like THE
FIGHTER and you see the last reel you’re

00:05:42.539 --> 00:05:47.889
like how are those the same movie but if you
progressively change your movie as you go

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:55.139
it will just happen without you being jarred
but yeah I love using color to tell a story

00:05:55.139 --> 00:06:00.890
but that was from getting a production designer
and wardrobe too you start to realize in THE

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:05.919
WEDDING SINGER everybody is like color coded
(the same thing in The Ridiculous 6 each town

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:08.669
had a color palette.

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:14.760
It really gets tricky when there gets to be
hundreds of extras you really need a good

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.659
costume designer and you need a DP, costume
designer and producer designer all on the

00:06:19.659 --> 00:06:20.659
same page.

00:06:20.659 --> 00:06:27.199
That’s the thing that I love about doing
bigger budget movies you have just more control

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:33.000
over everything and it’s funny after I did
the indie I had the best time because I had

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.770
no one telling me what I should and shouldn’t
do.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:40.210
It was literally my final cut and then I was
like man I’m really itching to do a big

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.900
movie again and I’m back now.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:47.970
My next movie is going to be THE LEGEND OF
SINBAD but then I’m like but with that comes

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.159
all the politics too so it’s always a give
and take.

00:06:51.159 --> 00:06:54.530
Film Courage: And the 1980’s was so colorful.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.030
Even thinking back to [the TV show] DESIGNING
WOMEN and they all had the great outfits and

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.439
shoulder pads and it was so colorful.

00:07:00.439 --> 00:07:05.561
Frank: And you know what is was for me - the
color palette in that movie - I think in the

00:07:05.561 --> 00:07:09.200
eighties I think felt manmade (it was like
the 1950’s too).

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.280
I was like no 1970’s colors - no browns,
no green.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:17.870
Everything was like fuchsia and purple and
electric blue because that’s my image of

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:18.870
the 1980’s.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:23.300
All the sudden we’re making everything and
everybody’s haircuts were cut.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:24.449
People didn’t have beards.

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:27.500
If they did it would be more trimmed.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.069
Film Courage: There were colored stonewashed
jeans.

00:07:30.069 --> 00:07:32.199
I had purple and turquoise.

00:07:32.199 --> 00:07:36.919
Frank: Everything felt like people did stuff
to everything.

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:38.691
It didn’t just grow out of the ground.

00:07:38.691 --> 00:07:38.710
Question for the viewers: What are the big
lessons you learned from this video?

